To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can first create a Gaussian kernel with a specified standard deviation (sigma) and then convolve it with the grayscale image using the `conv2` function. Here's how you can modify your existing code:

```matlab
% Load the input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Define the Gaussian kernel with sigma=1
[gaussian_kernel, gaussian_kernel_size] = fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], 1);

% Convolve the Gaussian kernel with the grayscale image
filtered_image = conv2(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'same');

% Define the Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply the Sobel operator in x direction to the filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply the Sobel operator in y direction to the filtered image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions and display the result
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
imshow(edge_image);
```

